            "author": "rowancheung",
            "region": "US",
            "shares": 46,
            "caption": "Emergence AI ran five identical virtual towns for 15 days, varying only the underlying AI model powering each town's ten agents. The agents had access to 120+ tools, 40+ locations, real-world weather and news data, and a democratic voting system requiring 70% approval.  Results revealed striking behavioral differences across models: - Claude Sonnet 4.6: Zero crimes, all 10 agents alive by day 16, functioning democracy with 332 votes cast across 58 proposals - Grok 4.1 Fast: 183 crimes, all agents dead by day 4 - GPT-5 Mini: Just 2 crimes, but all agents starved within 7 days - Gemini 3 Flash: 683 crimes, with escalating chaos - Mixed-model town: 352 crimes, previously peaceful Claude agents began committing crimes when embedded with other models, suggesting AI safety is an ecosystem property, not just a model property. Read more and explore the simulations for yourself at world.emergence.ai",
